    "name": "Personal Cartography",
    "summary": "Create a five-point route through your learning. Show how your explanation of techno-racism changed across the term and what you will carry into future study, work, or technology use.",
    "html": "<p><br></p><p style=\"margin:0;line-height:1.6;\">Worth 15% of your final course grade. Maximum score: 15 points.</p><p><br></p><p style=\"margin:0;line-height:1.6;\">BFS218, Fall 2026. Weight: 15 percent.</p><p><br></p><h3>Your task</h3><p><br></p><p style=\"margin:0;line-height:1.6;\">Create a five-point route through your learning. Show how your explanation of techno-racism changed across the term and what you will carry into future study, work, or technology use.</p><p><br></p><h3>Required route points</h3><p><br></p><ul style=\"margin:0;padding-left:28px;\"><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">An early observation you could notice but not yet explain.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">A concept or case that changed your interpretation.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">A before-and-after comparison using dated course items.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">A question or limit that your analysis does not fully resolve.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">One realistic future practice and why it matters.</li></ul><p><br></p><h3>Format</h3><p><br></p><p style=\"margin:0;line-height:1.6;\">Choose a five-part written route of about 500 to 700 words, or a simple map with a three-to-four-minute narration and transcript. A numbered text route is as acceptable as a visual. Use at least four dated course items from both halves of the term. Private disclosure is not required.</p><p><br></p><h3>Build on earlier feedback</h3><p><br></p><p style=\"margin:0;line-height:1.6;\">Before you start, revisit the feedback on Assessment 4. Choose one suggestion that can help your reasoning or use of evidence here. You do not need a grade or feedback on an assignment that is still being marked.</p><p><br></p><h3>How your work will be graded</h3><p><br></p><p style=\"margin:0;line-height:1.6;\">Strong means 85 to 100 percent of a criterion, Clear means 70 to 84 percent, Developing means 50 to 69 percent, and Limited means 0 to 49 percent.</p><p><br></p><h3>Course-long evidence: 4.5 points</h3><p><br></p><ul style=\"margin:0;padding-left:28px;\"><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Strong: Four or more dated course items create a convincing route across both halves.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Clear: Required course items are present and connected.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Developing: Artefacts are thin, clustered, or weakly connected.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Limited: The route lacks required course evidence.</li></ul><p><br></p><h3>Change in understanding: 3.75 points</h3><p><br></p><ul style=\"margin:0;padding-left:28px;\"><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Strong: Before-and-after thinking is specific, honest, and supported.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Clear: A clear change in understanding is shown.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Developing: Change is claimed but weakly demonstrated.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Limited: No meaningful development is shown.</li></ul><p><br></p><h3>Critical analysis: 3.75 points</h3><p><br></p><ul style=\"margin:0;padding-left:28px;\"><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Strong: Concepts, evidence, limits, and unresolved tensions are integrated with depth.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Clear: Analysis is accurate and addresses a meaningful tension.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Developing: Analysis is mostly descriptive or misses limits.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Limited: Analysis is unsupported or substantially inaccurate.</li></ul><p><br></p><h3>Communication and future practice: 3 points</h3><p><br></p><ul style=\"margin:0;padding-left:28px;\"><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Strong: The map is coherent, accessible, and ends with a realistic practice tied to learning.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Clear: The route is clear and the future practice fits.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Developing: The route or practice is vague.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Limited: The final product is difficult to follow or lacks a future practice.</li></ul><p><br></p><p style=\"margin:0;line-height:1.6;\">Your grade is based on the evidence in your work. Personal disclosure, polished production, and technical effects do not replace course learning.</p><p><br></p><h3>How to submit in Blackboard</h3><p><br></p><ul style=\"margin:0;padding-left:28px;\"><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Open this assignment and select Start attempt. Write or paste your written work into the Submission editor using the headings in these instructions. No Word or PDF upload is required. Type or paste your work directly into Blackboard. Use headings and a numbered text pathway instead of a diagram if you prefer.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">If you choose a visual format, insert it with a text description or attach it as an accessible file. If you choose an audio or video format, attach the recording and captions or a transcript. A working Seneca OneDrive or Microsoft Stream link is also acceptable.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Keep all required parts together in this assignment. Before submitting, check that your text, attachments, and links open correctly.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Select Submit, wait for Blackboard’s confirmation, and keep the receipt. If you save a draft instead, return to submit it before the deadline.</li></ul><p><br></p><h3>Your work and permitted tools</h3><p><br></p><p style=\"margin:0;line-height:1.6;\">Use your own observations, choices, explanations, and sources. Do not use generative AI to write or rewrite your submission, invent experiences or evidence, or create narration in your place. You may use spelling, dictation, screen-reader, captioning, and accessibility tools that do not generate the course content. Ask me before using a tool if you are unsure.</p><p><br></p><h3>Save a draft, then submit once</h3><p><br></p><p style=\"line-height:1.6;\">Work directly in Blackboard. Use Save and Close when you need a break, then reopen the same attempt to continue. Saving a draft does not submit it. You do not need to finish in one sitting.</p><p><br></p><p style=\"line-height:1.6;\">Before you select Submit, check that every required answer and supporting item is included. You have one submitted attempt and cannot edit it afterward. If you submit the wrong work or encounter a genuine problem, contact me promptly through Faculty Contact Information. I can review whether an individual additional attempt is appropriate; it is not automatic. Approved accommodations and exceptions still apply.</p><p><br></p><h3>Deadlines and submission window</h3><p><br></p><p style=\"margin:0 0 16px;line-height:1.6;\">Released: November 26, 2026 at 12:01 a.m. Eastern.</p><p><br></p><p style=\"margin:0 0 16px;line-height:1.6;\"><strong>Due: December 11, 2026 at 11:59 p.m. Eastern.</strong></p><p><br></p><p style=\"margin:0 0 16px;line-height:1.6;\">No automatic 48-hour window applies to this final assessment. Routine extension requests must arrive before the posted due date.</p><p><br></p><p style=\"margin:0 0 16px;line-height:1.6;\">Work submitted after the posted deadline receives zero unless an extension or exception is approved. Unexpected emergencies and approved accommodations are considered separately.</p><p><br></p><p style=\"margin:0 0 16px;line-height:1.6;\"><a href=\"deadline-policy.html\">Read the full Deadlines and Extensions policy</a>.</p>",
